---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Timeline from '../components/Timeline.astro';
import ResearchLandscape from '../components/ResearchLandscape.astro';
import Highlights from '../components/Highlights.astro';
import Publications from '../components/Publications.astro';

const selectedSlugs = ['counterfactual-self-reports', 'decoded-but-unused', 'path-already-walked', 'fyi-factcheck'];
import { publications } from '../data/publications';
import { building } from '../data/building';
const selectedPubs = selectedSlugs.map(s => publications.find(p => p.slug === s)!);
---
<Base>
  <!-- Hero / About -->
  <section id="about">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="hero-grid">
        <img src="/profile.png" alt="Phongsakon Mark Konrad" class="hero-photo" />
        <div>
          <h1 class="hero-name">Phongsakon <span class="brand-mark">Mark</span> Konrad</h1>
          <p class="hero-tag">anti big data big data guy · how and why models learn, and how to make them trustworthy</p>
          <div class="about">
            <p>A sushi chef learning his cuts, except the fish is a model. I take models apart by hand to see what is really happening inside.</p>
            <p>I am currently a Research Collaborator at <a href="https://www.sdu.dk/en/forskning/cis/laboratories/data-and-intelligence-lab">SDU's Data and Intelligence Lab</a> and at the <a href="https://hkust.edu.hk/">HKUST</a> DataVISards group, TA for AI at SDU (where I built an <a href="https://phomarkon.github.io/ai101/#/welcome" target="_blank" rel="noopener">interactive AI course</a>), and Founder of SaturoLabs. I have been admitted to the <a href="https://www.mlmi.eng.cam.ac.uk/">MPhil in Machine Learning and Machine Intelligence at the University of Cambridge</a>, which I begin this October.</p>
            <p class="rq-heading">It all comes down to one question.</p>
            <blockquote class="rq-quote">Does the learning process that scaling relies on actually install internal representations capable of genuine counterfactual reasoning, or only representations that imitate it? And if it is imitation, what would a learning process have to do differently to install the real thing?</blockquote>
            <p>The north star is genuine machine intelligence, systems that understand the world and work out its mechanisms the way science taught us to. I am not sure the dominant paradigm gets there. Prediction at scale seems to buy better and better imitation while real understanding stays out of reach, benchmarks measure the wrong thing, and scaling laws have never quite sat right with me. So I go looking for first principles in the physics of learning instead.</p>
            <p>So I study the two things that give understanding away. <strong>Counterfactual reasoning</strong>, the cleanest line between understanding and pattern matching, through <a href="/publications/counterfactual-self-reports">identifiability tests on counterfactual reports</a>. And the <strong>structure of learned representations</strong>, their geometry and identifiability, through <a href="/publications/routing-subspaces">subspace audits</a>, <a href="/publications/decoded-but-unused">causal tracing</a>, and <a href="/publications/embedding-confound">probe direction audits</a>. Causal representation learning is home territory.</p>
            <p>I work phenomenon first. I would rather exhibit one clean, surprising regularity than derive it from axioms, and I would take a small shot at something field shifting over a safe incremental result. From here my goal is a PhD. I do not yet know which fork I will spend years on, whether scale simply cannot buy understanding or whether the thing that matters has to come from structure elsewhere, and I am fine with that for now. I am following the curiosity, and the questions keep getting sharper.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="hero-social">
            <a href="mailto:phongsakon@outlook.dk">phongsakon@outlook.dk</a>
            <a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=zPHjLREAAAAJ&hl=en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Scholar ↗</a>
            <a href="https://github.com/phomarkon" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub ↗</a>
            <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/phongsakonmarkkonrad" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn ↗</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Selected Work -->
  <section id="selected-work">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Selected Work</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">A few peer reviewed papers that best represent my current research direction.</p>
      <div class="sw-section">
        {selectedPubs.map((p, i) => (
          <a class="sw-card" href={`/publications/${p.slug}`}>
            <span class="sw-num">{i + 1}</span>
            <div>
              <div class="sw-head">
                <span class="sw-title">{p.title}</span>
              </div>
              <div class="sw-authors">{p.authors}</div>
              <div class="sw-meta">
                <span class={`sw-venue-badge ${p.status === 'Under Review' ? 'submitted' : p.status === 'Accepted' ? 'accepted' : ''}`}>{p.status === 'Under Review' ? p.status : `${p.venue} · ${p.status}`}</span>
              </div>
              <p class="sw-tldr">{p.oneLine}</p>
            </div>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Highlights -->
  <section id="highlights">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Highlights</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">News, talks, and achievements, most recent first.</p>
      <Highlights />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Career Timeline -->
  <section id="timeline">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <h2>Career Timeline</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">Where I've studied and worked, in chronological order.</p>
      <Timeline />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Research Landscape -->
  <section id="research">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <h2>Research Landscape</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">Three modalities, one through line. Hover a paper to see its neighbours light up. Click to open its page.</p>
      <ResearchLandscape />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Building -->
  <section id="building">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Building</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">Tools, a course, and products I have shipped. Code where it is open.</p>
      <div class="build-grid">
        {building.map((b) => (
          <article class="build-card">
            <h3 class="build-title">{b.title}</h3>
            <p class="build-blurb">{b.blurb}</p>
            <div class="build-meta">{b.meta}</div>
            <div class="build-links">
              {b.links.map((l) => (
                <a href={l.href} target={l.href.startsWith('/') ? undefined : '_blank'} rel="noopener">{l.label}</a>
              ))}
            </div>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Publications -->
  <section id="publications">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Publications</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">Peer reviewed and accepted work first, then preprints.</p>
      <Publications />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
